---
// What this is, where it came from, and what a reader should not trust.
import Shell from "../layouts/Shell.astro";
import { LANGS, LANG_NAMES, href, index, years } from "../site";

const site = index();
const span = years(site.papers);
const draft = new Set(site.draft_langs);

const counted = LANGS.map((l) => ({
  lang: l,
  full: site.papers.filter((p) => p.status[l] === "full").length,
  stub: site.papers.filter((p) => p.status[l] === "stub").length,
}));

const restricted = site.papers.filter((p) => p.access === "restricted").length;
---

<Shell title="About" here="about" narrow>
  <h1>What this is</h1>

  <p>
    A hundred papers that built computing, read off the published PDFs and set
    as text: {site.papers.length} of them, {span[0]} to {span[1]}. Each one
    keeps its formulas, its figures, its listings and its bibliography, and
    each one is translated into Vietnamese, Chinese and Japanese. The point is
    to be able to read the original work in the language you think in, with
    the mathematics still readable.
  </p>

  <h2>What is machine written</h2>

  <p>
    All of it, except the papers themselves. The text was got off each PDF by a
    tool: the text layer where the PDF had a usable one, a layout model or a
    vision model over the page images where it did not. The translations were
    written by a language model against a glossary of terms that is fixed for
    the whole corpus, so that one term is one term in every paper. Nothing here
    was translated by a person and nothing here was checked by one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every page says at the foot of it how it was made and which model made it.
    Where a page or a section was written by a model small enough that the
    result is doubtful, or went through a gateway rather than the model it was
    meant for, the page says that too, where you are reading rather than only
    at the top. Read a translation against the English if it matters.
  </p>

  <ul class="facts">
    {
      counted.map((c) => (
        <li>
          {LANG_NAMES[c.lang]}: {c.full} full
          {c.stub > 0 ? `, ${c.stub} abstract only` : ""}
          {draft.has(c.lang) ? ", draft" : ""}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    A draft language is one whose glossary does not yet cover enough of the
    terms in the corpus. It is still published, with the page saying what it
    is, because hiding it would be the same corpus with less of it visible and
    no more of it true.
  </p>

  <h2>Licences</h2>

  <p>
    The papers belong to their authors and their publishers. Nothing here
    republishes a PDF: where a paper is linked, it is linked to the page the
    publisher put it on. What is published here is the text and the figures,
    and only where the licence allows it.
  </p>

  <p>
    A paper in the public domain, or under a licence that allows
    redistribution, is here in full. {
      `${restricted} ${restricted === 1 ? "paper is" : "papers are"}`
    } under a licence that does not, and {restricted === 1 ? "is" : "are"} here
    as front matter and a short abstract with a link to the publisher, which is
    what quotation allows and no more. A paper whose licence nobody has
    established is not here at all, in any form, until somebody establishes it.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is made</h2>

  <p>
    Two repositories. <a href="https://github.com/tamnd/papers">tamnd/papers</a>
    is the corpus: Markdown, figures, manifests and reports, and never a PDF.
    <a href="https://github.com/tamnd/papers-reader">tamnd/papers-reader</a> is
    the toolchain that reads the PDFs, translates them, audits the result
    against ninety-two numbered rules and builds this site. Both are public and
    both take corrections.
  </p>

  <p>
    A paper that should be here and is not can be proposed as an issue on the
    toolchain. So can a translation that is wrong, which is the more useful of
    the two: a wrong term in the glossary is wrong in a hundred papers at once,
    and one report fixes all of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={href("")}>Back to the papers</a>.
  </p>
</Shell>
